---
// Side-by-side: same request, two wire formats.
const codecRows = [
  { hex: "01 00 00 04", note: "vocab=gpt-4 / role=user", ctrl: true },
  { hex: "00 00 0F A1", note: '"What"' },
  { hex: "00 00 09 BE", note: '" is"' },
  { hex: "00 00 04 21", note: '" the"' },
  { hex: "00 00 1C 33", note: '" capital"' },
  { hex: "00 00 02 5A", note: '" of"' },
  { hex: "00 00 1F 90", note: '" France"' },
  { hex: "00 00 02 30", note: '"?"' },
];

const jsonSample = `POST /v1/chat/completions HTTP/2
content-type: application/json

{
  "model": "gpt-4",
  "messages": [
    { "role": "user",
      "content": "What is the capital of France?" }
  ]
}`;

const codecHeader = `POST /v1/chat HTTP/2
content-type: application/codec`;
---
<section id="compare" class="section compare">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">on the wire</p>
    <h2>Same payload. Different planet.</h2>
    <p class="compare__lede">
      An LLM doesn't read words. It reads token IDs. The JSON envelope,
      the UTF-8 string, the per-character escaping: that's all shipping bytes
      the model immediately throws away. Codec ships what the model actually
      speaks, whether the sender is a human typing a prompt or
      another model handing off a context.
    </p>

    <div class="compare__grid">
      <article class="card">
        <header class="card__head">
          <span class="card__tag card__tag--neutral">JSON</span>
          <span class="card__sub">~ 142 bytes &middot; text</span>
        </header>
        <pre class="code"><code>{jsonSample}</code></pre>
        <footer class="card__foot">
          Re-tokenized on the server. UTF-8 to tokens to UTF-8 to UTF-8 to
          tokens again. Every hop pays the same tax.
        </footer>
      </article>

      <article class="card card--codec">
        <header class="card__head">
          <span class="card__tag card__tag--data">CodeC</span>
          <span class="card__sub">32 bytes &middot; binary</span>
        </header>
        <pre class="code"><code><span class="dim">{codecHeader}</span>{"\n\n"}{codecRows.map((r) => (
  <span class="row">
    <span class={r.ctrl ? "hex hex--ctrl" : "hex hex--data"}>{r.hex}</span>
    <span class="hex-note">  {r.note}</span>
    {"\n"}
  </span>
))}</code></pre>
        <footer class="card__foot">
          Token IDs that the model already speaks. One control word at the
          head names the vocab and message role. That's the only
          framing.
        </footer>
      </article>
    </div>

    <p class="compare__split-note">
      v0.4.1 separates two numbers that prior cuts conflated. <strong>§1 protocol-only</strong> is the synthetic-stream bench (known token-ID corpora run through Codec's encoder + compression libs locally, no engine, no model). <strong>§1b engine-output</strong> is what each engine actually emits at temperature&nbsp;0; ratios depend on what the model decides to generate, not just the protocol. Both numbers are real; they answer different questions.
    </p>

    <ul class="compare__bullets">
      <li>
        <span class="bullet-num">4.8&times;<br/>to&thinsp;392&times;</span>
        <span class="bullet-body">
          <strong>§1 protocol-only</strong>, synthetic-stream wire reduction at 2&nbsp;K msgpack across token-distribution profiles:
          <strong>4.8&times;</strong> on uniform random (worst case),
          <strong>16.6&times;</strong> on low-entropy 50-unique-IDs,
          <strong>392&times;</strong> on cyclic period-10 (best case).
          Same library, no engine in the loop. This is the protocol's contribution alone.
          <a href="https://github.com/wdunn001/Codec/blob/main/packages/bench/results/2026-05-15T20-00-00Z/synthetic/wire.json" rel="noopener">wire.json &rarr;</a>
        </span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="bullet-num">1,707&times;<br/>3,868&times;</span>
        <span class="bullet-body">
          <strong>§1b engine-output</strong> at 2&nbsp;K msgpack+dict-zstd, what each engine actually produces:
          <strong>1,707&times;</strong> sglang, <strong>137&times;</strong> vllm, <strong>3,868&times;</strong> llama.cpp fp16. Ratios scale with how compressible the engine's emitted token sequence is at temp&nbsp;0; vllm's sampler emits less compressible output, hence the lower number despite the same protocol.
          <a href="#benchmarks">Receipts &rarr;</a>
        </span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="bullet-num">24/24<br/>24/24</span>
        <span class="bullet-body">
          <strong>Cross-stack unanimity (new in v0.4.1).</strong> 6 client implementations
          (TS/Web, Python, .NET, Rust, Java, C) &times; 3 engines (sglang, vllm, llama.cpp) = 24 cells per engine. Every cell is <strong>wire-unanimous</strong> (clients receive byte-identical bytes) AND <strong>decode-unanimous</strong> (clients produce byte-identical IDs after decompressing). The decode side is what caught 5 of 6 silent dict-zstd failures in v0.4.0.
        </span>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .compare__lede { max-width: 64ch; margin-bottom: 2.5rem; }

  .compare__grid {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1rem;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
  }
  @media (min-width: 880px) {
    .compare__grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; }
  }

  .card {
    background: var(--bg-elev);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 10px;
    overflow: hidden;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
  }
  .card--codec { border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--data) 50%, var(--border)); }
  .card__head {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    padding: 0.75rem 1rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
  }
  .card__tag {
    padding: 0.125rem 0.5rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    border: 1px solid var(--border-strong);
    color: var(--fg-muted);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
  }
  .card__tag--data {
    background: var(--data-soft);
    border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--data) 60%, transparent);
    color: var(--data);
  }
  .card__sub { color: var(--fg-faint); }

  .code {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 1.25rem 1.25rem 1rem;
    overflow-x: auto;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--fg);
  }
  .code code { white-space: pre; display: block; }

  .dim { color: var(--fg-faint); }
  .row { display: contents; }
  .hex { font-weight: 600; }
  .hex--ctrl { color: var(--control); }
  .hex--data { color: var(--data); }
  .hex-note { color: var(--fg-faint); }

  .card__foot {
    padding: 0.875rem 1.25rem 1rem;
    color: var(--fg-faint);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    background: var(--bg);
  }

  .compare__split-note {
    margin: 2.5rem 0 1.25rem;
    padding: 0.875rem 1rem;
    color: var(--fg-muted);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--data) 4%, transparent);
    border-left: 3px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--data) 50%, transparent);
    border-radius: 0 6px 6px 0;
  }
  .compare__split-note strong { color: var(--fg); font-weight: 600; }

  .compare__bullets {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 1rem;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
  }
  @media (min-width: 720px) {
    .compare__bullets { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); }
  }
  .compare__bullets li {
    display: flex;
    gap: 1rem;
    align-items: baseline;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    background: var(--bg-elev);
    padding: 1rem 1.125rem;
    border-radius: 8px;
  }
  .compare__bullets li:nth-child(1) {
    border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--control) 35%, var(--border));
  }
  .compare__bullets li:nth-child(1) .bullet-num { color: var(--control); }
  .compare__bullets li strong { color: var(--fg); font-weight: 600; }
  .compare__bullets li a {
    color: var(--data);
    border-bottom: 1px dotted var(--border-strong);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
  }
  .compare__bullets li a:hover { border-bottom-color: var(--data); }
  .bullet-num {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--data);
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    line-height: 1.15;
    /* Two-line stats like "1,707× / 3,868×" need a slightly wider gutter
       than the prior 5.5rem single-line ones. */
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    min-width: 6.5rem;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .bullet-body {
    color: var(--fg-muted);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }
</style>
